---
/**
 * RevealScript — arms the `.sr` scroll-reveal system (see styles/reveal.css).
 *
 * Two scripts, and the split is the point.
 *
 * The first is `is:inline` so it is not bundled, deferred or module-scoped: it runs
 * synchronously at the position it appears in the document. Placed above the revealed
 * sections, it sets `sr-ready` before those elements are parsed, so they are never
 * painted visible and then yanked hidden. Inlining it also means it cannot be delayed
 * behind a module graph, which is the window a flash would live in.
 *
 * It arms the offset state ONLY if IntersectionObserver exists to later remove it.
 * Setting the class first and feature-detecting afterwards would leave a browser
 * without the observer stuck on a permanently offset page.
 *
 * There is a load-time reveal for what is already on screen, but still no no-scroll
 * timeout. The distinction matters and the two are not variations of one idea.
 *
 * The timeout revealed the WHOLE page after a fixed delay, so anyone who paused on the
 * hero for 2.5s before scrolling — most readers — then scrolled through a page where
 * nothing animated. It is not coming back.
 *
 * The load-time pass only touches elements already within the first viewport, and it is
 * required rather than optional: the reveal fades as well as lifts, and the trigger line
 * sits at 75% of the viewport, so without it the bottom quarter of the landing view would
 * render blank until the reader scrolled.
 *
 * The second is an ordinary bundled module that does the observing. If it never runs,
 * `sr-ready` is set but nothing is hidden yet either, because CSS only hides elements
 * carrying `.sr`, and the worst case is a page that renders exactly as it does today
 * with motion turned off.
 */
---

<script is:inline>
  if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
    document.documentElement.classList.add('sr-ready');
  }
</script>

<script>
  // Reveal once and stop watching. Re-hiding on scroll-up means a reader who scrolls
  // back to re-read a paragraph watches it fade out from under them.
  const targets = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.sr');
  if (targets.length && 'IntersectionObserver' in window) {
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries, obs) => {
        for (const e of entries) {
          if (!e.isIntersecting) continue;
          e.target.classList.add('in');
          obs.unobserve(e.target);
        }
      },
      // The top margin is enormous ON PURPOSE, and it is the whole correctness argument.
      //
      // IntersectionObserver reports a CHANGE in intersection, not a state. With a plain
      // `0px 0px -12% 0px` an instant scroll — a TOC click, a scrollbar drag, End, or
      // landing on a #hash — moves an element from below the viewport to above it between
      // two samples. Old state: not intersecting. New state: not intersecting. No callback
      // is ever delivered, the element keeps its offset forever, and because reveal is the
      // only thing that unobserves, nothing later rescues it. Measured on this page: one
      // jump to the bottom left 21 of 26 elements permanently 34px low, which reads as
      // overlapping text and as "the animation doesn't work". Adding the HomeRail made it
      // routine, since every rail click is exactly that jump.
      //
      // Extending the root 10^6px upward makes "above the viewport" a permanently
      // intersecting region, so the only transition that exists is below → intersecting.
      // An element can be scrolled past arbitrarily fast and still lands inside it.
      //
      // The bottom margin decides WHERE on screen the reveal plays, which turned out to
      // matter as much as whether it fires at all. At -12% the trigger line sat at 88% of
      // viewport height: measured over a full scroll, 25 of 27 reveals began in the bottom
      // 20% of the screen and were finished before the reader's eye reached them. The
      // animation was running correctly and being seen by nobody.
      //
      // -25% moves the line to 75%, so the fade and lift play in the lower-middle of the
      // screen, in front of the reader rather than off the bottom edge.
      { rootMargin: '1000000px 0px -25% 0px', threshold: 0 },
    );
    for (const t of targets) io.observe(t);

    // Anything already ON SCREEN at load is revealed immediately, without waiting for a
    // scroll. This is mandatory now that the reveal fades rather than only lifting.
    //
    // The trigger line sits at 75% of the viewport, so an element occupying the bottom
    // quarter of the first screen is visible to the reader but has NOT met the observer's
    // condition. While the reveal was transform-only that was harmless — the element was
    // merely 34px low. With opacity in play it would render the bottom quarter of the
    // landing view blank until the reader happened to scroll, which is a worse bug than
    // the one this whole system exists to animate away.
    //
    // Deliberately `rect.top < innerHeight` rather than the trigger line: the guarantee
    // worth having is the strong one, that nothing on screen at load is ever invisible.
    // Elements above the line would be revealed by the observer's initial callback in the
    // same tick anyway, so this only ever acts earlier, never differently.
    const vh = window.innerHeight;
    for (const t of targets) {
      if (t.getBoundingClientRect().top < vh) {
        t.classList.add('in');
        io.unobserve(t);
      }
    }

    // Print is the one case worth forcing: a print job never scrolls, and a 20px offset
    // repeated down a page reads as sloppy typesetting rather than as motion.
    window.addEventListener('beforeprint', () => {
      for (const t of targets) {
        t.classList.add('in');
        io.unobserve(t);
      }
    });
  }
</script>
